$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM N-Cbz-, N-Moc-and N-Fmoc-Protected Proline Derivatives 2f-i, 2k and 2l;
General Procedure The solvent and the reagents (including equivalents and
the order of addition) are given in Table 2. In general, a suspension of
L-proline (1; 9-11 mmol, 1 equiv) and Et3N (or DIPEA in the case of 2l)
in CHCl3 ([1] = 0.3 mol/L) was cooled to -20 °C and treated very slowly
with a soln of PG-Cl (PG = Cbz, Moc, Fmoc) in CHCl3 ([PG-Cl] = 2 mol/L).
The cooling bath was removed and the mixture was stirred at r.t. for
0.5-1 h; full conversion into 7b-d was indicated by formation of a clear
solution. Then, the reaction mixture was cooled to 0 °C, treated with
additional reagents (see Table 2, ‘Reagents’ column) and stirred at r.t.
overnight. For workup, the reaction mixture was either filtered with
EtOAc-cyclohexane (1:1) through a layer of silica gel or diluted with
MTBE (and filtered, when DCC was used), washed with 1 M HCl, sat. NaHCO3
soln and brine, dried over MgSO4 and concentrated under reduced pressure.
All products were purified by column chromatography on silica gel
(EtOAc-cyclohexane; for 2l: MeOH-EtOAc, 1:50). Methyl
N-(Benzyloxycarbonyl)-L-prolinate (2f; Table 2, Entry 1) Yield: 1.75 g
(76%); pale yellow oil. [α]D20 -59.3 (c 1.34, CHCl3) [Lit.1f [α]D20 -54.6
(c 0.48, CHCl3)]. IR (ATR): 3030, 2976, 1744, 1698, 1606, 1445, 1406,
1349 (vs), 1198, 1166, 1116, 1086, 1000 (s) cm-1. 1H NMR (300 MHz,
CDCl3): δ = 7.34-7.26 (m, 5 H, C6H5), 5.18-4.99 (m, 2 H, CH2Ph),
4.37/4.32 (2 × dd, J = 8.8, 3.1, 8.9, 3.5 Hz, 1 H, NCH), 3.71-3.41 (m, 5
H, NCH2, OCH3), 2.27-2.11 (m, 1 H, NCHCHH), 2.03-1.80 (m, 3 H,
NCHCHHCH2). 13C NMR (75 MHz, CDCl3): δ = 173.2/173.0 [CH(CO)],
154.8/154.2 [N(CO)], 136.6/136.5 (CH2CPh), 128.3/127.8/127.7 (5 × CHPh),
66.9/66.8 (OCH2), 59.0/58.7 (OCH3), 52.1/52.0 (NCH), 46.8/46.3 (NCH2),
30.8/29.8 (NCHCH2), 24.2/23.4 (NCH2CH2). LRMS (EI): m/z (%) = 263 (1)
[M+], 204 (25), 160 (25), 91 (100).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM optimization study, optimized on solvent
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Practical one-pot double functionalizations of proline
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Huy, Peter; Schmalz, Hans-Guenther
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Synthesis (2011), (6), 954-960

